NNAAMMEE
     bbrraannddeellff — mark an ELF binary for a specific ABI

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     bbrraannddeellff [--llvv] [--ff _E_L_F___A_B_I___n_u_m_b_e_r] [--tt _s_t_r_i_n_g] _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The bbrraannddeellff utility marks an ELF binary to be run under a certain ABI
     for FreeBSD.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff _E_L_F___A_B_I___n_u_m_b_e_r
             Forces branding with the supplied ELF ABI number.  Incompatible
             with the --tt option.  These values are assigned by SCO/USL.

     --ll      Writes the list of all known ELF types to the standard error.

     --vv      Turns on verbose output.

     --tt _s_t_r_i_n_g
             Brands the given ELF binaries to be of the _s_t_r_i_n_g ABI type.  Cur‐
             rently supported ABIs are “FreeBSD”, “Linux”, and “SVR4”.

     _f_i_l_e    If --tt _s_t_r_i_n_g is given it will brand _f_i_l_e to be of type _s_t_r_i_n_g,
             otherwise it will simply display the branding of _f_i_l_e.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     Exit status is 0 on success, and 1 if the command fails if a file does
     not exist, is too short, fails to brand properly, or the brand requested
     is not one of the known types and the --ff option is not set.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     The following is an example of a typical usage of the bbrraannddeellff command:

           brandelf file
           brandelf -t Linux file

